---
import {
  GitCompareIcon,
  Key01Icon,
  Link01Icon,
  Link04Icon,
  SearchList01Icon,
  UserGroupIcon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import CodeBlock from '@/components/CodeBlock.astro'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'Keyword gap analysis', text: 'The searches your competitors rank for and you don’t, compared with your own Search Console.', href: '/features/keyword-gap', icon: GitCompareIcon, link: 'Keyword gap analysis' },
  { title: 'Backlink gap analysis', text: 'The sites that link to two or more of your competitors and not to you.', href: '/features/backlink-gap', icon: Link01Icon, link: 'Backlink gap analysis' },
  { title: 'seo research', text: 'Every research action, its inputs and how to run it from the CLI.', href: '/docs/research', icon: SearchList01Icon },
  { title: 'Competitor keyword research', text: 'Look up what any domain ranks for in your browser, with no account.', href: '/tools/competitor-keyword-research', icon: UserGroupIcon, link: 'Free tool' },
  { title: 'Backlink checker', text: 'The sites that link to any domain, in your browser.', href: '/tools/backlink-checker', icon: Link04Icon, link: 'Free tool' },
  { title: 'API keys', text: 'Add a DataForSEO or Ahrefs key to the hosted MCP or the CLI.', href: '/docs/keys', icon: Key01Icon },
]
---

<FeaturePage
  title="SEO competitor analysis"
  metaTitle="SEO competitor analysis tool for AI agents"
  description="Find your real SEO competitors, the pages that earn their traffic and the sites that link to them and not you, from your own DataForSEO or Ahrefs key."
  path="/features/seo-competitor-analysis"
  lead="Find the sites that compete with you in Google, the pages that earn their traffic and the sites that link to them and not to you. Your agent runs each step from your own DataForSEO or Ahrefs key and shows the numbers it used."
  {related}
  relatedHeading="Read more"
>
  <h2>What an SEO competitor analysis answers</h2>
  <p>
    An SEO competitor analysis compares your site with the sites that rank
    for the same Google searches. It answers three questions: who ranks for
    the searches you want, which of their pages earn the traffic, and which
    sites link to them and not to you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your SEO competitors are often not the companies you sell against. A
    software company can lose its best searches to a publisher, a forum or a
    tool directory, and those are the sites to study first.
  </p>

  <h2>Plausible's 10 closest search competitors include 5 SEO sites</h2>
  <p>
    We ran this page's first step on plausible.io, the privacy-friendly web
    analytics product, with DataForSEO for the US on 11 November 2026.
    DataForSEO counted 15,475 domains that share at least one ranking keyword
    with plausible.io. These were the 10 with the most shared keywords, with
    each site's Domain Rating by{' '}
    <a href="https://ahrefs.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ahrefs</a>:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Shared keywords (DR)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>google.com</td><td>653 (DR 100)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>semrush.com</td><td>590 (DR 92)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>medium.com</td><td>535 (DR 94)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>backlinko.com</td><td>481 (DR 90)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>hubspot.com</td><td>474 (DR 93)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ahrefs.com</td><td>436 (DR 91)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>neilpatel.com</td><td>436 (DR 91)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>monsterinsights.com</td><td>423 (DR 83)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>seranking.com</td><td>386 (DR 86)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>matomo.org</td><td>369 (DR 92)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Besides Google, only 2 of the 10 sell website analytics: MonsterInsights,
    a Google Analytics plugin for WordPress, and Matomo. Five are SEO tools or SEO
    blogs. Simple Analytics, a direct rival, shares 127 keywords and sits
    far down the list.
  </p>
  <p>
    The top pages for plausible.io show the likely reason. Its backlinks
    guide ranks for 170 keywords, including "backlinks" (4,400 US searches a
    month) in sixth place, and those are exactly the searches Semrush, Ahrefs
    and Backlinko compete for. The home page carries the business: by
    DataForSEO's estimate it earns 1,193 of the site's 1,808 monthly visits
    from search (66%).
  </p>
  <p>
    The lesson works for any site: a competitor list counts shared keywords,
    so read it next to your own top pages. A site that only shares one
    off-topic guide with you is not a competitor worth a strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>Set up competitor analysis in your agent</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Add the hosted MCP server to Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, ChatGPT or any
      client with remote MCP, and sign in with Google. The commands are at
      the top of this page.
    </li>
    <li>
      Save a <a href="/docs/keys">DataForSEO or Ahrefs key</a> on your
      account page. The provider bills you directly, and every DataForSEO
      result says what the request cost.
    </li>
    <li>Ask in plain words, like the prompts in the steps below.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Prefer a terminal? <code>npm i -g seo</code> then <code>seo start</code>{' '}
    gives you the same data through the <a href="/docs/research">seo research</a>{' '}
    command, with the key kept on your machine. Each step below shows the
    prompt and the command.
  </p>

  <h2>Run an SEO competitor analysis in 5 steps</h2>

  <h3>1. Find your SEO competitors</h3>
  <CodeBlock value={`Who are the SEO competitors of example.com in the US? Group them into direct rivals, publishers and tools.`} wrap />
  <CodeBlock value={`seo research competitors --target example.com --country us`} wrap />
  <p>
    The competitors list gives each domain's shared keywords, its average
    position on them and its own keyword count. Your own site and platforms
    that share keywords with almost everyone, such as YouTube, Reddit and
    Wikipedia, are left out, and the result names each one it dropped. For
    a quick read on any one of them, ask for a{' '}
    <a href="/features/domain-overview">domain overview</a>: its estimated
    traffic, top pages, competitors and links in one answer.
  </p>

  <h3>2. See which pages earn their traffic</h3>
  <CodeBlock value={`Show the top pages of example.org and say what each one is: guide, tool, comparison or product page.`} wrap />
  <CodeBlock value={`seo research top_pages --target example.org --country us`} wrap />
  <p>
    Top pages rank a site's pages by estimated search traffic, with how many
    keywords each page ranks for in first place, second or third, and fourth
    to tenth. Add a trailing slash to read one section, such as{' '}
    <code>example.org/blog/</code>. The page types tell you what Google
    rewards in your market better than any keyword list.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Read what one competitor page ranks for</h3>
  <CodeBlock value={`What does https://example.org/blog/pricing-guide rank for, and which of those searches could one of my pages win?`} wrap />
  <CodeBlock value={`seo research ranked_keywords --target https://example.org/blog/pricing-guide`} wrap />
  <p>
    A full URL reads that one page. Each row has the keyword, its position,
    its monthly searches and its difficulty, so you can see the long tail a
    single strong page collects.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Check who ranks for the searches you want</h3>
  <CodeBlock value={`Show the top 10 for "invoice software for freelancers" and "freelance invoice app" with each site's Domain Rating, and say whether they need one page or two.`} wrap />
  <CodeBlock value={`seo research serp --keywords "invoice software for freelancers,freelance invoice app" --depth 10
seo research serp_overlap --keywords "invoice software for freelancers,freelance invoice app"`} />
  <p>
    The SERP lists who ranks today for up to 5 keywords, each with its
    domain's authority, and the sites an AI Overview cites. A first page full
    of sites with a lower Domain Rating than yours is a better target than a
    low difficulty score. SERP overlap counts how many top 10 pages and sites
    each pair of searches shares. DataForSEO often returns Google redirect
    links without the exact page, so compare sites with it, or use Ahrefs
    when you need pages.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Find the sites that link to them and not you</h3>
  <CodeBlock value={`Run a link gap for example.com against example.org, example.net and competitor.example. Which sites link to two or more of them and not to me?`} wrap />
  <CodeBlock value={`seo research link_gap --target example.com --competitors example.org,example.net,competitor.example`} wrap />
  <p>
    Link gap reads each site's top 1,000 referring domains and lists the
    sites that link to at least two of your competitors and not to you,
    highest Domain Rating first. URL shorteners, link lists and site-stats
    directories link to almost everyone, so they are left out and named. To
    see what earns a competitor its links, ask for{' '}
    <code>pages_by_backlinks</code>, and for one site's full list ask for{' '}
    <code>backlinks</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Link gap works best between sites of a similar size. When one site has
    more than 1,000 referring domains, the list stops where the read stopped
    rather than guess below it. The{' '}
    <a href="/features/backlink-gap">backlink gap analysis</a> page has a
    worked run and how to read the result.
  </p>

  <h2>Compare their keywords with yours in a keyword gap</h2>
  <p>
    Steps 1 to 3 show what competitors rank for. The next question is which
    of those searches you are missing. The{' '}
    <a href="/features/keyword-gap">keyword gap analysis</a> page shows how
    your agent compares each competitor's ranked keywords with your own
    Search Console queries, with a worked example. For a quick look at one
    competitor's keywords without an account, use the free{' '}
    <a href="/tools/competitor-keyword-research">competitor keyword research</a>{' '}
    tool.
  </p>

  <h2>What competitor data can and cannot show</h2>
  <p>
    Every number on this page is a provider estimate from DataForSEO's or
    Ahrefs' own index, and the two providers count keywords and traffic
    differently, so compare competitors within one provider. Shared keywords
    say two sites appear for the same searches, not that they sell the same
    thing. An empty list means the provider has nothing for that site in its
    database. When you want to know what your own pages actually get, your
    Search Console is the number to trust, and your agent keeps it apart from
    the estimates.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
